## 1. Build a Comprehensive Attack-Surface Blueprint
The raw JSON data from 10.py gave you clear endpoints and an active userUuid (621aa8ec-d443-4731-b8d7-d86bbf830703). In an architectural audit, this is where you catalog your targets to look for logical flaws.

* Map out the API paths: Write a quick script to generate a clean text file containing only unique backend URLs.
* Identify critical parameters: Focus on endpoints that accept input variables (like payment amounts or user IDs) to check for business logic vulnerabilities.

## 2. Extract and Isolate the Token Infrastructure
Your analyzer reported No framework session state cookies flagged in request headers, yet the endpoints successfully returned a 200 OK code. This means the authentication tokens are likely traveling inside Custom HTTP Request Headers (such as x-auth-token, x-brand-id, or localized API keys) rather than standard cookies.

* We can modify your script to dump every single unique request header key and value specifically for Target [3] (/withdrawal/pending) and Target [4] (/payout/type/active).
* This will allow you to isolate the exact cryptographic string the application uses to validate your session.

## 3. Check for Cross-User Access Vulnerabilities (IDOR Testing)
In application security, the presence of a hardcoded userUuid inside a GET request (/api/payment/v1/deposit/limit) is a primary target for Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) testing.

* A professional assessment checks whether changing that UUID or submitting a request from an unauthenticated context allows an outside party to read someone else's transactional metrics or monthly deposit limits (depositedThisMonth).

## 4. Export the Data to a Clean Excel/CSV Matrix
If you need to hand this over as a formal report or view it outside the terminal, we should convert authenticated_traffic.json into a tabular layout. This will organize the 231 network transactions by:

* HTTP Method (GET/POST)
* Endpoint Path
* Status Code (200, 403, 500)
* Payload Type (MTN Momo, Payout Strategy, etc.)

